title: "Assessment: Done Right"
authors: Piotr Mitros
~~~~

Finding the Company
-------------------

Jay: Dad, what are you doing?

Piotr: Job hunting. We just moved to Poland, remember? I can't take my old job with me.

Jay: What kind of job?

Piotr: Something in education technology. I found this company called "2 Hour Learning." They're doing AI-powered personalized learning with mastery-based progression.

Jay: That sounds like what you've been working on forever.

Piotr: Exactly. They have two roles that fit: Director of Academics and SVP of Technical Product Management. Either would be interesting.

--- pause ---

Jay: So... did you apply?

Piotr: I did. And their application process is fascinating. They use AI-powered assessments to screen candidates.

Jay: That's kind of cool, right? You always say assessment should come first.

assessment_sidebar -> testing_first_context

Piotr: It *is* cool. Assessment-driven selection is a strong approach. Let me show you what happened though.

The Academics Track
-------------------

Jay: What went wrong?

Piotr: Well, for the Director of Academics role, their LLM evaluates whether you have the right background. I have five years as Chief Scientist of edX—that's the MIT/Harvard online learning initiative. Then many years managing research at ETS.

Jay: ETS... the testing company?

Piotr: Educational Testing Service. They make the SAT, GRE, TOEFL. They're *the* organization for educational assessment research. I led teams developing next-generation assessments.

Jay: So you know assessment.

Piotr: Intimately. But my Ph.D. is in electrical engineering, not education. The LLM apparently decided that a half-decade running education research at MIT/Harvard, plus years at the world's leading assessment organization, doesn't count as "equivalent to a Ph.D. in education."

--- pause ---

Jay: That seems... wrong?

assessment_sidebar -> validity_construct

Piotr: It's a construct validity problem. The system is measuring "has credential X" rather than "can do job Y." Those correlate, but they're not the same thing.

Jay: What about the other job?

The Technical Track
-------------------

Piotr: The SVP of Technical Product Management role had a different screening—a coding assessment.

Jay: That should be easy for you.

Piotr: The assessment itself was well-designed. Good problems, reasonable time limits. But I hit a bug in their test harness. My code was correct, but their validation had an issue.

Jay: What did you do?

Piotr: There's no way to contact anyone. It's fully automated. No human in the loop.

assessment_sidebar -> human_fallback

--- pause ---

Jay: So you're stuck?

Piotr: For that assessment, yes. But here's the thing—I kept going. The assessments are genuinely interesting. Well-designed questions. Good use of AI for feedback. Real thought went into them.

Jay: So... good tests, but bad process?

Piotr: Exactly. This is what I want to talk about. Let's dig into what makes assessment work—and what goes wrong when it's deployed without the right safeguards.

The Standards Gap
-----------------

assessment_sidebar -> standards_overview

Piotr: There are professional standards for educational and psychological testing. The AERA/APA/NCME Joint Standards. They represent decades of research on how to do assessment ethically and validly.

Jay: What do they say?

Piotr: A few key principles apply here. First: **high-stakes decisions require multiple sources of information**. One automated screen shouldn't determine everything.

Jay: Their system uses just the AI.

Piotr: Right. Second: **there must be mechanisms for review and appeal**. When the test has an error—or the algorithm does—there needs to be a human who can look at it.

Jay: And there's no one to contact.

--- pause ---

Piotr: Third: **fairness requires transparency**. Test-takers should understand how they'll be evaluated. "An LLM will decide if your background counts" isn't transparent.

assessment_sidebar -> campbell_activity

Jay: So they're violating the standards?

Piotr: They're building something that could be great, but deploying it before it's ready. The individual assessments are clever. The system architecture ignores hard-won knowledge about how assessment can go wrong.

The Irony
---------

Jay: Wait—they have a Spiky POV about this, don't they?

Piotr: "In a fully automated system, automating quality control is 90% of the work and 90% of the value."

Jay: But their own hiring system doesn't have quality control.

Piotr: That's the irony. They understand the principle.

--- pause ---

assessment_sidebar -> decision_theory

Piotr: Cronbach—one of the founders of modern testing—said we should ask: "Valid for what decision?" A test might be valid for screening out clearly unqualified candidates but invalid for distinguishing between highly qualified candidates.

Jay: And you're highly qualified.

Piotr: That's not for me to say. But the point is: **assessment works when it's done right**. The testing effect is real. Backward design is powerful. Data-driven instruction helps students.

Jay: So you agree with them?

Piotr: On the core insight? Absolutely. Assessment should drive learning. But the details matter. Validity, reliability, fairness—these aren't bureaucratic box-checking. They're what separates assessment that helps from assessment that harms.

Stealth Assessment
------------------

assessment_sidebar -> stealth_context

Jay: You've worked on this stuff, right?

Piotr: For years. At edX, we pioneered using the "exhaust" of learning for assessment. Every interaction a student has—every problem attempt, every video pause, every forum post—contains information about what they understand.

Jay: So you don't need separate tests?

Piotr: You still need calibration. But you can reduce the need for artificial test situations. Assessment woven into learning feels less threatening and captures richer data.

--- pause ---

Jay: That sounds like what they're trying to do.

Piotr: It is! That's why I find them interesting despite the friction. The vision is right. I just want them to debug it before it screens out people who could help them build it right.

assessment_sidebar -> reflection_activity

Jay: So what now?

Piotr: Now? I'm going to build a course that shows what I know. Not just tell them I have experience—demonstrate it. Using their own tools, ironically.

Jay: Is that what this is?

Piotr: That's exactly what this is.
